from pathlib import Path
import json

ROOT=Path(__file__).parent
blocks=[]
def step(title,text,latex,art,alt):
    return dict(title=title,text=text,latex=latex,art=art,alt=alt)
def block(id,lesson,title,intro,steps,summary,placement,optional=False):
    blocks.append(dict(id=id,lesson=lesson,title=title,intro=intro,steps=steps,summary=summary,placement=placement,optional=optional))

block('01-wave-graphs','U5L1','Worked example: Read wave graphs',
'Two graphs describe the same wave. Use the spatial pattern and the motion at one point to find amplitude, wavelength, frequency, and speed.',[
step('Read the position graph','The crests are at 0.20 m and 0.80 m. The largest displacement from equilibrium is 3.0 cm. Measure wavelength between consecutive crests, not from a crest to a trough.',r'\lambda=0.80-0.20=0.60\,\mathrm{m}\qquad A=3.0\,\mathrm{cm}','wave:1','Position graph with consecutive crests at 0.20 and 0.80 metres and amplitude 3 centimetres.'),
step('Read and convert the period','At one fixed point, consecutive crests arrive 150 milliseconds apart. Convert that period to seconds before using it in a calculation.',r'T=150\,\mathrm{ms}\left(\frac{1\,\mathrm{s}}{1000\,\mathrm{ms}}\right)=0.150\,\mathrm{s}','wave:2','Time graph with consecutive crests separated by 150 milliseconds.'),
step('Calculate frequency and speed','Frequency counts complete cycles per second. Multiply frequency by wavelength to find how quickly the pattern travels.',r'f=\frac{1}{T}=\frac{1}{0.150\,\mathrm{s}}=6.67\,\mathrm{Hz}\\v=f\lambda=(6.67\,\mathrm{Hz})(0.60\,\mathrm{m})=4.00\,\mathrm{m/s}','wave:3','Linked position and time graphs emphasize one complete spatial cycle and one complete time cycle.'),
step('Check what each value means','The wave advances one wavelength during one period. Its amplitude is 3.0 cm, while the distance from trough to crest is 6.0 cm. These are different measurements.',r'v=\frac{\lambda}{T}=\frac{0.60\,\mathrm{m}}{0.150\,\mathrm{s}}=4.00\,\mathrm{m/s}','wave:4','Travelling wave with separate arrows for amplitude, full crest-to-trough height, and one wavelength.')],
'Use a position graph for wavelength and a time graph for period. Read the axes before choosing an equation.','Section 2: after frequency, period, and wave-speed definitions.')

block('02-superposition','U5L1','Worked example: Add overlapping pulses',
'Two pulses overlap on a rope. At the marked point, one would displace the rope 3.0 cm upward and the other 2.0 cm downward. Find the combined displacement.',[
step('Choose a positive direction','Choose upward as positive. Measure both displacements from the same equilibrium line at the same position and instant.',r'y_1=+3.0\,\mathrm{cm}\qquad y_2=-2.0\,\mathrm{cm}','super:1','Two separate pulse contributions at one marked location, upward and downward from equilibrium.'),
step('Add the signed displacements','The principle of superposition adds displacements, including their signs. It does not add their absolute sizes.',r'y=y_1+y_2=(+3.0)+(-2.0)=+1.0\,\mathrm{cm}','super:2','Aligned contribution bars show an upward displacement partly cancelled by a downward displacement.'),
step('Interpret the result','The positive answer places the rope 1.0 cm above equilibrium at this point. This is partial destructive interference.',r'y=+1.0\,\mathrm{cm}','super:3','The combined pulse lies above equilibrium at the marked point.'),
step('Test complete cancellation','If the downward contribution were 3.0 cm, the instantaneous displacement at the point would be zero. The pulses continue through each other; zero displacement is not proof that all wave energy vanished.',r'y=(+3.0)+(-3.0)=0\,\mathrm{cm}','super:4','Signed pulse displacements are compared at the same position to illustrate superposition.')],
'Add signed displacements at the same place and time. Cancellation describes the instantaneous displacement.','Section 3: directly before or after the interference interactive.')

block('03-sound-intensity','U5L2','Worked example: Sound spreads with distance',
'A small sound source produces an intensity of 8.0 × 10⁻⁴ W/m² at 2.0 m. Find the intensity at 6.0 m. Assume spherical spreading, with negligible absorption and reflections.',[
step('Identify the two distances','Compare the same source at two locations. Its power stays constant while the area over which that power spreads increases.',r'r_1=2.0\,\mathrm{m}\qquad r_2=6.0\,\mathrm{m}\\I_1=8.0\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{W/m^2}','intensity:1','Source at the center of concentric spherical-wave cross sections, with receivers at 2 and 6 metres.'),
step('Use the inverse-square relationship','Intensity is power per unit area. A sphere has area proportional to the square of its radius, so intensity decreases as the square of distance increases.',r'\frac{I_2}{I_1}=\left(\frac{r_1}{r_2}\right)^2\qquad I_2=I_1\left(\frac{r_1}{r_2}\right)^2','intensity:2','Equal amounts of outgoing energy spread across a small and a larger spherical surface.'),
step('Substitute and calculate','The distance becomes three times as large. Square the distance ratio before multiplying by the starting intensity.',r'I_2=(8.0\times10^{-4})\left(\frac{2.0}{6.0}\right)^2=8.9\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{W/m^2}','intensity:3','Receivers on the inner and outer wavefronts, with the outer surface divided into more equal areas.'),
step('Check the ratio','Tripling the distance produces one-ninth the intensity in this model. This does not mean the sound is perceived as exactly one-ninth as loud.',r'\frac{I_2}{I_1}=\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^2=\frac{1}{9}','intensity:4','Nine equal area tiles illustrate how the same outgoing power is distributed over nine times the area.')],
'For a fixed point-like source in open space, intensity follows an inverse-square relationship. Reflections and absorption can change real measurements.','Section 1: after sound intensity and distance.')

block('04-pipe-harmonics','U5L2','Worked example: Design two pipes for one note',
'Design an open pipe and a pipe closed at one end with the same 170 Hz fundamental. Use a sound speed of 340 m/s and ignore end correction.',[
step('Draw the fundamental patterns','These curves show air displacement. An open end is a displacement antinode; a closed end is a displacement node. The open pipe contains half a wavelength, and the stopped pipe contains a quarter.',r'L_{\mathrm{open}}=\frac{\lambda}{2}\qquad L_{\mathrm{stopped}}=\frac{\lambda}{4}','pipes:1','Open pipe with displacement antinodes at both ends and stopped pipe with one displacement node and one antinode.'),
step('Find the shared wavelength','Both pipes make the same frequency in the same air. Their wavelengths therefore match even though their lengths differ.',r'\lambda=\frac{v}{f}=\frac{340\,\mathrm{m/s}}{170\,\mathrm{Hz}}=2.00\,\mathrm{m}','pipes:2','Fundamental standing-wave patterns in an open pipe and a pipe closed at one end show their different required lengths.'),
step('Calculate each length','Use the fraction of a wavelength that fits each fundamental pattern. For the same fundamental, the stopped pipe is half as long.',r'L_{\mathrm{open}}=\frac{2.00}{2}=1.00\,\mathrm{m}\\L_{\mathrm{stopped}}=\frac{2.00}{4}=0.500\,\mathrm{m}','pipes:3','Open pipe of 1.00 metre and stopped pipe of 0.500 metre drawn to the same length scale.'),
step('Check the next allowed mode','An ideal open pipe supports every positive integer harmonic. An ideal pipe closed at one end supports only odd harmonics. Its next allowed mode is the third harmonic.',r'f_{2,\mathrm{open}}=2(170)=340\,\mathrm{Hz}\\f_{3,\mathrm{stopped}}=3(170)=510\,\mathrm{Hz}','pipes:4','Next displacement modes: full wavelength in the open pipe and three quarters of a wavelength in the stopped pipe.')],
'Start with the boundary conditions, then choose the wavelength-to-length relationship. Distinguish the next allowed mode from the second harmonic.','Section 2: before Design the Perfect Instrument.')

block('05-resonance-data','U5L2','Worked example: Analyze resonance measurements',
'A tube closed at one end resonates in its fundamental at 500 Hz with an air-column length of 17.0 cm. Find the sound speed, then analyze three trial results. Ignore end correction.',[
step('Convert the measured length','The fundamental of a stopped tube contains one-quarter wavelength. Convert centimetres to metres so the calculated speed will be in metres per second.',r'L=17.0\,\mathrm{cm}=0.170\,\mathrm{m}\\\lambda=4L=4(0.170)=0.680\,\mathrm{m}','resonance:1','Ruler beside a stopped air column marks 17.0 centimetres, with a quarter-wave displacement pattern.'),
step('Calculate the trial speed','Use the measured wavelength and the driving frequency. This result is an estimate based on this trial.',r'v=f\lambda=(500\,\mathrm{Hz})(0.680\,\mathrm{m})=340\,\mathrm{m/s}','resonance:2','Sound source drives the stopped tube while the resonant displacement pattern is highlighted.'),
step('Average repeated trials','Suppose three trials give speeds of 338, 340, and 342 m/s. Average the trial speeds rather than choosing only the closest result.',r'\bar v=\frac{338+340+342}{3}=340\,\mathrm{m/s}','resonance:3','Three trial markers cluster on a speed scale around a central mean marker.'),
step('Compare with the reference','Using 343 m/s as the reference, divide the absolute difference by the reference value. This is percentage error or deviation from a reference, not symmetric percent difference.',r'\mathrm{error}=\frac{|340-343|}{343}\times100\%=0.875\%\approx0.9\%','resonance:4','Measured mean and reference speed shown close together on a magnified horizontal scale.')],
'Convert units, calculate each trial, average the estimates, then compare with the stated reference. Keep enough digits until the final result.','Section 2: before the resonance-tube application.')

block('06-light-frequency-energy','U5L3 / U5L5','Worked example: From wavelength to photon energy',
'Light in vacuum has a wavelength of 500 nm. Find its frequency and the energy of one photon. Use c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s and h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s.',[
step('Convert nanometres to metres','The speed of light is in metres per second, so first express wavelength in metres.',r'\lambda=500\,\mathrm{nm}\left(\frac{10^{-9}\,\mathrm{m}}{1\,\mathrm{nm}}\right)=5.00\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{m}','photon:1','A green wave with one wavelength bracketed and a nanometre scale.'),
step('Calculate frequency','Rearrange the wave-speed relationship before substituting. A shorter vacuum wavelength would correspond to a higher frequency.',r'c=f\lambda\quad\Rightarrow\quad f=\frac{c}{\lambda}\\f=\frac{3.00\times10^8}{5.00\times10^{-7}}=6.00\times10^{14}\,\mathrm{Hz}','photon:2','Wave cycles pass a fixed detector; the marked wavelength and detector are emphasized.'),
step('Calculate one photon’s energy','Multiply frequency by Planck’s constant. The result describes one photon, not the entire light beam.',r'E=hf=(6.626\times10^{-34})(6.00\times10^{14})=3.98\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm{J}','photon:3','A single photon event is highlighted from a stream of identical green photon symbols.'),
step('Separate photon energy from beam energy','If twice as many photons of this wavelength arrive, the total energy doubles. The frequency and energy of each photon stay the same.',r'E_{\mathrm{total}}=NE_{\mathrm{photon}}\qquad E_{2N}=2E_N','photon:4','Two equal-time streams contain different numbers of identical-color photon symbols.')],
'Wavelength sets frequency in vacuum, and frequency sets energy per photon. Photon count also matters when comparing total beam energy.','U5L3 section 1 for wave conversion; U5L5 section 1 if photon-energy calculation is introduced there.')

block('07-real-image','U5L3','Worked example: Find a real lens image',
'A 3.0 cm tall object is 30 cm from a converging lens with focal length +20 cm. Find the image distance, magnification, and image height using the thin-lens model.',[
step('Assign the signs','Use a positive focal length for a converging lens and a positive object distance for this real object. The object is outside the focal point, so expect a real image.',r'f=+20\,\mathrm{cm}\qquad d_o=+30\,\mathrm{cm}\qquad h_o=+3.0\,\mathrm{cm}','lens-real:1','Converging lens with an upright object outside the left focal point; both focal points are labeled.'),
step('Rearrange the thin-lens equation','Isolate the reciprocal of image distance. Keep every distance in centimetres for this calculation.',r'\frac{1}{f}=\frac{1}{d_o}+\frac{1}{d_i}\quad\Rightarrow\quad\frac{1}{d_i}=\frac{1}{f}-\frac{1}{d_o}','lens-real:2','A parallel principal ray refracts through the far focal point, and a central ray continues straight.'),
step('Solve for distance and size','Take the reciprocal to obtain image distance. Then calculate magnification and multiply it by the object height.',r'\frac{1}{d_i}=\frac{1}{20}-\frac{1}{30}=\frac{1}{60}\quad\Rightarrow\quad d_i=+60\,\mathrm{cm}\\m=-\frac{60}{30}=-2.0\qquad h_i=(-2.0)(3.0)=-6.0\,\mathrm{cm}','lens-real:3','Principal rays meet at an inverted image on the far side, twice the object height.'),
step('Interpret and check','Positive image distance means a real image on the opposite side of the lens. Negative magnification means inverted. Its magnitude of 2 means twice as tall. A screen placed at the image can capture it.',r'd_i>0:\ \mathrm{real}\qquad m<0:\ \mathrm{inverted}\qquad |m|=2.0','lens-real:4','A screen at the real image intercepts the converging rays and displays the inverted object.')],
'The result is a real, inverted image 60 cm from the lens, with height 6.0 cm and magnification −2.0. The ray diagram and signs agree.','Section 2: after ray rules, before the thin-lens application.')

block('08-virtual-image','U5L3','Worked example: Find a virtual lens image',
'Move the same 3.0 cm object to 10 cm from the converging lens. Its focal length is still +20 cm. Determine how the image changes.',[
step('Predict from the object position','The object now lies inside the focal length. Transmitted rays diverge, so expect an upright virtual image on the object side.',r'f=+20\,\mathrm{cm}\qquad d_o=+10\,\mathrm{cm}\qquad d_o<f','lens-virtual:1','An upright object lies between a converging lens and its near focal point.'),
step('Solve the lens equation','Use the same equation and sign convention as for the real image. A negative answer is meaningful, not automatically an error.',r'\frac{1}{d_i}=\frac{1}{20}-\frac{1}{10}=-\frac{1}{20}\quad\Rightarrow\quad d_i=-20\,\mathrm{cm}','lens-virtual:2','Outgoing rays diverge; dashed backward extensions meet on the object side.'),
step('Calculate the magnification','The two minus signs give positive magnification. Multiply by the object height to find the upright image height.',r'm=-\frac{d_i}{d_o}=-\frac{-20}{10}=+2.0\\h_i=mh_o=(+2.0)(3.0)=+6.0\,\mathrm{cm}','lens-virtual:3','The virtual image is upright and twice the height of the object; dashed rays locate it.'),
step('Use a physical check','The image is virtual because the actual transmitted rays do not meet there. Moving a screen cannot capture this image. If the object were exactly at the focal point, the outgoing rays would be parallel.',r'd_i<0:\ \mathrm{virtual}\qquad m>0:\ \mathrm{upright}','lens-virtual:4','Diverging transmitted rays and dashed backward extensions locate an upright virtual image.')],
'The virtual image is 20 cm from the lens on the object side, upright, and twice as tall. Dashed backward extensions locate it.','Section 2: immediately after the real-image example.')

block('09-refraction','U5L3','Optional worked example: Calculate a refracted angle',
'Light enters glass from air at 45.0° to the normal. Use refractive indices 1.00 for air and 1.50 for glass. Find the refracted angle.',[
step('Measure from the normal','The normal is perpendicular to the boundary. The incident angle is measured between the incoming ray and that normal, not along the surface.',r'n_1=1.00\qquad n_2=1.50\qquad\theta_1=45.0^\circ','refraction:1','Air-glass boundary with a perpendicular dashed normal and an incoming ray at 45 degrees.'),
step('Rearrange Snell’s law','Divide by the second refractive index to isolate the sine of the refracted angle.',r'n_1\sin\theta_1=n_2\sin\theta_2\quad\Rightarrow\quad\sin\theta_2=\frac{n_1}{n_2}\sin\theta_1','refraction:2','Incident ray and normal are highlighted at the boundary, with the refracted direction left as a question.'),
step('Calculate the angle','Use degree mode. First calculate the sine value, then use inverse sine to recover an angle.',r'\sin\theta_2=\frac{1.00}{1.50}\sin45.0^\circ=0.4714\\\theta_2=\sin^{-1}(0.4714)=28.1^\circ','refraction:3','Ray bends toward the normal in glass; the smaller refracted angle is marked.'),
step('Check the direction','The ray enters a higher-index material, so its speed decreases and it bends toward the normal. Its frequency remains fixed by the source; its wavelength decreases.',r'\theta_2<\theta_1\qquad v_2=\frac{c}{n_2}<v_1\qquad f_2=f_1','refraction:4','Wavefronts are closer together inside glass while the ray bends toward the normal.')],
'The refracted angle is 28.1° to the normal. An answer larger than 45° would contradict the expected direction here.','Section 2: optional quantitative extension after refractive index.',True)

block('10-wireless','U5L4','Worked example: Wavelength and signal delay',
'Find the wavelength of a 2.4 GHz carrier. Then estimate travel time for a satellite link with two specified 35,800 km legs. Treat propagation speed as 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s.',[
step('Convert the carrier frequency','Giga means one billion. Carrier frequency tells how many electromagnetic cycles occur per second; it does not by itself tell the data rate.',r'f=2.4\,\mathrm{GHz}=2.4\times10^9\,\mathrm{Hz}','wireless:1','Radio transmitter emits repeated wave cycles toward a receiver; one cycle is bracketed.'),
step('Calculate the wavelength','Divide propagation speed by frequency. The result is the spatial length of one carrier cycle.',r'\lambda=\frac{c}{f}=\frac{3.00\times10^8}{2.4\times10^9}=0.125\,\mathrm{m}=12.5\,\mathrm{cm}','wireless:2','One carrier wavelength is highlighted on a ruler between a transmitter and receiver.'),
step('Add the path and find delay','For this separate satellite example, the one-way ground-to-satellite-to-ground path contains both legs. Divide total path length by speed in matching units.',r'd=35{,}800+35{,}800=71{,}600\,\mathrm{km}\\t=\frac{d}{c}=\frac{71{,}600}{300{,}000}=0.239\,\mathrm{s}','wireless:3','Two ground stations communicate via one satellite; each specified path leg is labeled 35,800 kilometres, not to scale.'),
step('Check a return message','A return message along the same route doubles this propagation time. Processing adds further delay. Changing carrier frequency alone does not change the travel time in this model.',r't_{\mathrm{round\ trip}}=2t\approx0.477\,\mathrm{s}','wireless:4','The satellite relay shows outward and return paths in two colors, with a clock beside the receiver.')],
'Wavelength depends on frequency; propagation delay depends on path length and propagation speed. This specified path does not represent every satellite system.','Section 2: after comparing wireless systems.')

block('11-photoelectric','U5L5','Worked example: Can a photon eject an electron?',
'A hypothetical surface has a work function of 2.00 eV. Compare 500 nm light with 700 nm light, using E ≈ 1240/λ when wavelength is in nanometres and energy is in electronvolts.',[
step('Calculate the incident photon energy','Use the energy of one photon. The work function is the minimum energy needed to remove an electron from this surface.',r'E=\frac{hc}{\lambda}\approx\frac{1240\,\mathrm{eV\,nm}}{500\,\mathrm{nm}}=2.48\,\mathrm{eV}','photoelectric:1','A 500 nanometre photon approaches a metal surface with a labeled energy barrier.'),
step('Compare energy with the threshold','The photon has more energy than the work function. Emission is possible; the remaining energy can become electron kinetic energy.',r'E=2.48\,\mathrm{eV}>\phi=2.00\,\mathrm{eV}','photoelectric:2','Photon energy bar rises above the work-function threshold; an electron is shown at the surface.'),
step('Find maximum kinetic energy','Subtract the energy needed to remove the electron. This gives maximum kinetic energy, not the energy of every emitted electron.',r'K_{\max}=E-\phi=2.48-2.00=0.48\,\mathrm{eV}','photoelectric:3','An electron leaves the surface; an energy bar separates removal energy from the remaining kinetic energy.'),
step('Test a longer wavelength','At 700 nm, each photon has too little energy. Increasing photon flux still does not cause emission in this introductory single-photon model. The threshold depends on the material.',r'E_{700}\approx\frac{1240}{700}=1.77\,\mathrm{eV}<2.00\,\mathrm{eV}','photoelectric:4','Many red photon symbols approach the surface, but each energy bar remains below the threshold.')],
'A 500 nm photon can produce an electron with up to 0.48 eV of kinetic energy here. A 700 nm photon cannot overcome this surface’s work function.','Section 1: after the photoelectric evidence activity.')

block('12-spectral-shift','U5L5','Worked example: Calculate a spectral shift',
'A spectral line with a rest wavelength of 500 nm is observed at 505 nm. Find the fractional shift and estimate radial speed using the low-speed Doppler approximation.',[
step('Compare observed and rest wavelengths','The observed wavelength is longer. This is a redshift in the simple motion model. Keep both wavelengths in the same units.',r'\Delta\lambda=\lambda_{\mathrm{obs}}-\lambda_0=505-500=+5\,\mathrm{nm}','spectrum:1','Aligned rest and observed spectral strips show a line shifted from 500 to 505 nanometres.'),
step('Calculate the fractional shift','Divide the change by the rest wavelength. The nanometre units cancel, so redshift z is dimensionless.',r'z=\frac{\lambda_{\mathrm{obs}}-\lambda_0}{\lambda_0}=\frac{5}{500}=+0.010','spectrum:2','Rest and observed line positions are compared against the same wavelength scale.'),
step('Estimate radial speed','For speeds small compared with light speed, radial speed is approximately c times z. Use c ≈ 300,000 km/s. The positive sign indicates recession in this convention.',r'v_r\approx cz=(300{,}000\,\mathrm{km/s})(0.010)=+3{,}000\,\mathrm{km/s}','spectrum:3','Observer and source are separated by a line-of-sight arrow; the source moves away.'),
step('Check a second spectral line','All lines share the same fractional shift in this model. A rest line at 600 nm moves to 606 nm, not 605 nm. No detected shift would mean no detected radial motion, not necessarily no sideways motion.',r'\lambda_{\mathrm{obs}}=(1+z)\lambda_0=(1.010)(600)=606\,\mathrm{nm}','spectrum:4','Two pairs of spectral lines show corresponding shifts from 500 to 505 and from 600 to 606 nanometres.')],
'The source is receding at approximately 3,000 km/s along the line of sight. The approximation is appropriate for this small fractional shift.','Section 3: before the spectral-line-shift application.')

block('13-half-life','U5L5','Worked example: Calculate radioactive decay',
'An isotope has a half-life of 6 hours. Start with 80 mg of that isotope and find how much parent isotope remains after 18 hours.',[
step('Count the half-lives','Use the same time units in the elapsed time and half-life. The result counts how many repeated halvings occur.',r'n=\frac{t}{T_{1/2}}=\frac{18\,\mathrm{h}}{6\,\mathrm{h}}=3','decay:1','Timeline from zero to eighteen hours divided into three equal six-hour intervals.'),
step('Apply each halving','Each interval halves the parent isotope still present, not the original amount every time. Track the remaining parent mass.',r'80\,\mathrm{mg}\rightarrow40\,\mathrm{mg}\rightarrow20\,\mathrm{mg}\rightarrow10\,\mathrm{mg}','decay:2','Four parent-isotope population panels shrink by halves across the timeline.'),
step('Use the compact equation','The exponent counts half-lives. This gives the same result as the repeated-halving diagram.',r'M=M_0\left(\frac12\right)^n=80\left(\frac12\right)^3=10\,\mathrm{mg}','decay:3','Four population panels show the expected parent-isotope fraction halving at six-hour intervals.'),
step('Work backward and interpret','To reach one-eighth of the starting amount requires three halvings, so it takes 18 hours. Parent nuclei transform into products; the whole sample does not disappear. Small nucleus populations fluctuate around the expected counts.',r'\frac{10}{80}=\frac18=\left(\frac12\right)^3\qquad t=3(6\,\mathrm{h})=18\,\mathrm{h}','decay:4','Parent and daughter symbols remain in the sample; a tagged nucleus illustrates unpredictable individual decay.')],
'After 18 hours, 10 mg of the parent isotope remains. Half-life describes a statistical population pattern, not a scheduled decay time for each nucleus.','Section 2: after the half-life population activity.')

block('14-nuclear-bookkeeping','U5L5','Worked example: Balance nuclear changes',
'Use mass number A and atomic number Z to identify the products of alpha and beta-minus decay. Then connect a small rest-mass difference with released energy.',[
step('Track alpha decay','Uranium-238 emits an alpha particle, which contains two protons and two neutrons. Subtract 4 from mass number and 2 from atomic number to identify thorium-234.',r'{}^{238}_{92}\mathrm{U}\rightarrow{}^{234}_{90}\mathrm{Th}+{}^{4}_{2}\mathrm{He}\\238=234+4\qquad92=90+2','nuclear:1','Parent nucleus emits a cluster of two protons and two neutrons; daughter and alpha particle remain visible.'),
step('Track beta-minus decay','In carbon-14, a neutron changes into a proton while an electron and an electron antineutrino are emitted. Mass number stays 14, and atomic number rises to 7, producing nitrogen-14.',r'{}^{14}_{6}\mathrm{C}\rightarrow{}^{14}_{7}\mathrm{N}+{}^{0}_{-1}e+\bar\nu_e\\14=14+0\qquad6=7-1','nuclear:2','A neutron becomes a proton; emitted electron and antineutrino are drawn outside the nucleus.'),
step('Check gamma emission','An excited nucleus can emit a gamma photon without changing its proton or neutron counts. The nucleus loses excitation energy but keeps the same A and Z.',r'{}^{A}_{Z}\mathrm{X}^{*}\rightarrow{}^{A}_{Z}\mathrm{X}+\gamma','nuclear:3','The same nucleus before and after emits a gamma photon; proton and neutron counts stay unchanged.'),
step('Optional: connect mass and energy','Optional calculation: if the combined product rest masses are 0.0200 u lower than the initial rest masses, use the mass-energy conversion. Total mass-energy is conserved.',r'E=\Delta mc^2=(0.0200\,\mathrm{u})(931.5\,\mathrm{MeV/u})=18.6\,\mathrm{MeV}','nuclear:4','An energy ledger shows lower product rest mass balanced by energy carried away.')],
'Balance mass number and charge to identify nuclear products. Nuclear energy release can accompany a decrease in total rest mass, while total mass-energy remains conserved.','Section 2: after decay types; final quantitative energy step is extension material.')

block('15-timing-error','Honors','Honors worked example: Turn clock error into range error',
'A receiver has a timing error of 0.10 microseconds. Estimate the corresponding light-travel range error, then find the timing tolerance for a 10 m range-error budget.',[
step('Convert microseconds to seconds','Micro means one millionth. Convert before multiplying by a propagation speed in metres per second.',r'\Delta t=0.10\,\mu\mathrm{s}=0.10\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{s}=1.0\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{s}','timing:1','Signal emission and reception clocks have a small highlighted timing offset.'),
step('Calculate the range error','A timing error changes the inferred distance by speed times time error. Use the magnitude of the timing error for the error size.',r'|\Delta r|=c|\Delta t|=(3.00\times10^8)(1.0\times10^{-7})=30\,\mathrm{m}','timing:2','True receiver range and inferred range differ by a small highlighted segment.'),
step('Solve for the allowed timing error','Rearrange the relationship to meet a 10 m range-error budget. The clock error must be smaller than the calculated threshold to stay below the budget.',r'|\Delta t|<\frac{10\,\mathrm{m}}{3.00\times10^8\,\mathrm{m/s}}=3.33\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{s}=0.0333\,\mu\mathrm{s}','timing:3','A narrow acceptable timing band is shown beside a larger timing-error marker.'),
step('Compare and interpret','The original 0.10 μs error exceeds the timing tolerance and gives three times the allowed range error. Full navigation position error also depends on satellite geometry and estimation.',r'\frac{30\,\mathrm{m}}{10\,\mathrm{m}}=3','timing:4','Signal timing and the corresponding range discrepancy are shown together to connect a small clock error with a distance error.')],
'Even a small clock error can produce a substantial range error. Compare signed clock corrections first, then convert the residual timing error into a distance.','Before the local honors GPS/timekeeping applications; keep separate from the five core Rise lessons.',True)

ROOT.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
(ROOT/'process-blocks.json').write_text(json.dumps(blocks,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
print(f'Wrote {len(blocks)} blocks and {sum(len(b["steps"]) for b in blocks)} instructional steps.')
